  <hr class="rule">
  <h2>B. Rows only</h2>
  <p class="verdictline">The spec's own kill criterion, shipped as an option</p>
  <p class="note">If the diagram cannot answer "what does this rest on and which part is broken" in three seconds, it is decoration and the rows carry everything anyway. This is that version. Worth judging seriously rather than treating as the fallback: it loses nothing factual, it survives at any width, and it is the one that cannot embarrass us at 2px on a phone.</p>

  <p class="sizetag">Site width, 42rem</p>
  <div class="card site"><p class="eyebrow">The Whetstone · this question is mapped</p><p class="q">Is a nuclear pathway cheaper than a renewables-only one for Australia?</p><div class="rows"><div class="row"><span class="rnum">1</span><span class="rverdict">UNSTATED</span><span class="rtext">How often a reactor runs<span class="rfind"><span class="bang">! </span><b>Supplied.</b> Frontier needs 90 per cent uptime and never states it. GenCost prices 53 to 89 per cent, from coal’s own record.</span></span></div><div class="row"><span class="rnum">2</span><span class="rverdict">CONTESTED</span><span class="rtext">What the first plant costs<span class="rfind"><b>Factual.</b> Frontier removes the first-of-a-kind premium by the time nuclear is built. GenCost puts it above 100 per cent.</span></span></div><div class="row"><span class="rnum">3</span><span class="rverdict">CONTESTED</span><span class="rtext">How long the asset lives<span class="rfind"><b>Judgment call.</b> The same build cost spread over 50 years or recovered over 30. The choice is never argued for on either side.</span></span></div><div class="row"><span class="rnum">4</span><span class="rverdict">UNSTATED</span><span class="rtext">What the money could have earned instead<span class="rfind"><span class="bang">! </span><b>Supplied.</b> Frontier’s discount rate is undisclosed. The footnote that should name it reads “Error! Reference source not found.”</span></span></div></div><p class="foot">Read the map →</p></div>

